+
+// M33: definitions the PS4 build needs and its libraries leave out.
+
+// FFmpeg 7.1 (the sysroot's, built with --disable-everything and only the decoders PSP games use,
+// as for the rpcs3 port): the H.264 decoder's SEI code frees AV1 film grain parameters, whose
+// source isn't built. PSP movies never carry them, so there is nothing to free.
+void ff_aom_uninit_film_grain_params(void *s) {
+	(void)s;
+}
diff --git a/ps4/PS4TextDrawer.cpp b/ps4/PS4TextDrawer.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..970fbb6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps4/PS4TextDrawer.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,474 @@
+// Copyright (c) 2026- PPSSPP Project.
+
+// This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
+// it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
+// the Free Software Foundation, version 2.0 or later versions.
+
+// This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
+// but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
+// MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
+// GNU General Public License 2.0 for more details.
+
+// A copy of the GPL 2.0 should have been included with the program.
+// If not, see http://www.gnu.org/licenses/
+
+// M33: text on the PS4, through FreeType (PPSSPP's own copy, ext/freetype) and HarfBuzz
+// (ext/harfbuzz). Without a text drawer PPSSPP falls back to its small bitmap font, which only has
+// Latin letters: Russian, Japanese, Chinese and Korean menus, and game titles in them, came out as
+// "? ? ?".
+//
+// The UI font is PPSSPP's own (Roboto Condensed, from the assets: Latin, Turkish, Polish, Cyrillic,
+// Greek). A character it doesn't have comes from the first fallback that has it: the PS4's own
+// system fonts (/preinst/common/font: SSTJpPro, DFHEI5 for Chinese, SCEPS4Yoongd for Korean, SST),
+// the Noto Sans CJK fonts in the package (assets/fonts) where the system ones can't be read, and the
+// package's Noto Sans Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Khmer and Lao (and Symbols 2: ☐ ✕), which the PS4 doesn't have. The current
+// language's CJK font goes first, so Han characters take that language's shapes.
+//
+// HarfBuzz shapes each run of text in one font: Arabic letters joined, Thai, Khmer and Lao marks in
+// place. Lines with Arabic or Hebrew are put in order by a small version of the bidi rules: runs of
+// right-to-left text read right to left, numbers and Latin words in them left to right.
+// Bold fallback characters are FreeType's emboldening (the UI font has its own bold file).
+
